Why the win is so large at e ∈ {5, 6}:
  - libjxl gates `patches = true` at speed_tier <= kHare (effort >= 7).
    e5 and e6 stay at `patches = false` by default.
  - `EffortProfile::adapt_to_image_content` (W36-3 / W41-2 era) flips
    `patches = true` on `Screenshot`-class content at e ∈ {5, 6} —
    libjxl-superset behaviour the encoder has shipped for ages, but
    only fired when callers manually plumbed `with_content_class(Some(
    Screenshot))`. W44-164 wires the auto-classifier so the dispatch
    fires automatically.
  - Patches detection on screenshots typically catches repeated UI
    elements (icons, glyphs, button bars) and packs them into the
    reference frame. The 20-60% byte savings here matches the W36-3 /
    W41-2 measurements on the same corpus.

What changed from the W44-163 audit's "+37% screenshot bytes":
  - W44-163's "+37%" figure was from W36-3 / W41-2 at e7 (now byte-
    identical because patches is already on at e7+ by default).
  - The auto-classifier moves that lift down to e5/e6 where it's
    MORE valuable than at e7 (e5/e6 are the speed/quality tiers where
    patches-at-no-cost is most felt).
  - At e7+, the auto-classifier is a no-op (matches W23-1 / W23-2
    intent — only flip what isn't already on).

Streaming + animation paths:
  - Streaming `LossyEncoder` ingests pre-converted linear_rgb, so the
    raw sRGB u8 source the proxies need is not in scope. Auto-classifier
    returns None there (same precedent as W44-91).
  - Animation `encode_animation_lossy` could be wired to compute the
    proxies per-frame, but per-frame proxy cost would multiply across
    every frame and animation content is typically uniform across the
    timeline. Deferred to a follow-on chunk (see DO-NOT below).
  - Callers needing the auto-classifier on those paths can plumb
    `with_content_class(Some(class))` explicitly upstream.

DO NOT (future agents):
  - DO NOT raise the fcbr threshold above 0.40 — windows95 sits at
    0.360 in the gb82-sc corpus and IS a screenshot (patches helps
    pixel-art too).
  - DO NOT lower the fcbr threshold below 0.30 — that risks pulling
    297394-class photos (fcbr=0.0957) into the Screenshot bucket; the
    deadband [0.10, 0.35) is the safety margin.
  - DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any byte movement (per W44-66 user
    correction).
  - DO NOT re-investigate "should auto-classifier fire on streaming"
    without first adding the per-frame proxy compute to the streaming
    + animation paths.
  - DO NOT remove the `auto_classify_content_class_from_layout`
    short-circuit on `pixels < CONTENT_CLASS_MIN_PIXELS`. That short-
    circuit (a) saves the O(W·H) scan on throwaway-small inputs,
    and (b) keeps the 36/36 hash-lock fixtures byte-identical.

Follow-on chunks (NOT this chunk):
  - W44-165 candidate: thread per-frame proxy compute through animation
    encoder so screen-recordings get the auto-classifier lift.
  - W44-166 candidate: add streaming proxy compute (caller plumbs a
    `with_first_strip_proxies(...)` or similar shape) so live encoders
    can opt in to the dispatch.
  - W44-167 candidate: add 16-bit + linear-f32 layout coverage to
    `compute_w44_91_zenanalyze_proxies` so the auto-classifier extends
    to HDR + medical / scientific inputs.
